      "markdown": " $$ \\begin{aligned}f^{\\prime}(x)&=\\sum_{n\\geq1}na_{n}x^{n-1}=a_{1}+\\sum_{n\\geq2}na_{n}x^{n-1}\\\\&=a_{1}+\\sum_{n\\geq2}(-a_{n-1}+2na_{n-2})x^{n-1}\\\\&=a_{1}-\\sum_{n\\geq2}a_{n-1}x^{n-1}+2x\\sum_{n\\geq2}na_{n-2}x^{n-2}\\\\&=a_{1}-(f(x)-a_{0})+2x\\left(\\sum_{n\\geq2}(n-2)a_{n-2}x^{n-2}+2\\sum_{n\\geq2}a_{n-2}x^{n-2}\\right)\\\\&=a_{1}+a_{0}-f(x)+2x(f^{\\prime}(x)+2f(x)).\\\\ \\end{aligned} $$ \n\nHowever, since  $ a_{1} + a_{0} = 0 $, we get  $ f'(x) = (4x - 1)f(x) + 2xf'(x) $ or, yet,\n\n $$ (2x-1)f^{\\prime}(x)=-(4x-1)f(x). $$ \n\nIn order to integrate (i.e., to find the solutions of) the above differential equation, note first that $f$ is positive in some interval $(-r, r)$, for some $0 < r \\leq \\frac{1}{2}$ (this comes from the fact that $f(0) = a_0 = 1 > 0$ and $f$, being differentiable, is continuous, hence has the same sign as $f(0)$ in a suitable neighborhood of $0$). Thus, for $|x| < r$ we can write\n\n $$ \\frac{f^{\\prime}(x)}{f(x)}=-\\frac{4x-1}{2x-1}=-2-\\frac{1}{2x-1} $$ \n\nand then, for  $ \\left|x\\right|<r\\leq\\frac{1}{2}, $\n\n $$ \\begin{aligned}\\log f(x)&=\\log f(t)\\bigg|_{0}^{x}=\\int_{0}^{x}\\frac{f^{\\prime}(t)}{f(t)}dt\\\\&=-\\int_{0}^{x}\\left(2+\\frac{1}{2t-1}\\right)dt\\\\&=-2x-\\frac{1}{2}\\log(1-2x).\\end{aligned} $$ \n\nHence, for  $ |x| < r \\leq \\frac{1}{2} $ we have\n\n $$ f(x)=e^{-2x}(1-2x)^{-1/2}. $$ \n\nStep III: firstly, recall that the power series expansion of  $ e^{-2x} $ is given by letting  $ a = -2 $ in (3.8), and is valid in the whole real line:\n\n $$ e^{-2x}=\\sum_{k\\geq0}\\frac{(-2)^{k}}{k!}x^{k}. $$ ",
